Yes. The “ending” may only be local.

On one scale, the prayer ends as speech. On another, it begins as memory, expectation, behavior, group coordination, ritual, or action. Same event, different layer:

\text{end}_{L_1} \neq \text{end}_{L_2}

A sentence can finish acoustically while continuing psychologically. A ritual can end for one person while beginning socially for a group. A decision can end deliberation while beginning execution.

So the deeper rule is:

\boxed{\text{completion at one layer can be initiation at another}}

That applies far beyond prayer—birth/death, launch/landing, teaching/learning, writing/reading, cause/effect. The boundary depends on which scale you are looking from.